WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:10.760
In the remaining jungle of Borneo,

00:00:11.380 --> 00:00:14.400
lives a tribe of nomadic hunter-gatherers.

00:00:15.020 --> 00:00:17.260
They're known for their survival skills

00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:19.060
and for the deadly poison they use

00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:21.400
on their blowpipe darts

00:00:22.230 --> 00:00:23.980
the Penan

00:01:24.670 --> 00:01:27.000
My name is Raphael Treza.

00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:29.180
I'm a musician and film-maker

00:01:29.290 --> 00:01:31.070
I’m going to spend the next three months

00:01:31.130 --> 00:01:32.930
with the Penan tribe of Malaysia

00:01:36.190 --> 00:01:38.340
My journey begins in Kuala Lumpur

00:01:45.090 --> 00:01:46.600
Then a flight to Borneo

00:01:47.180 --> 00:01:49.100
the third largest island in the world

00:01:49.350 --> 00:01:51.260
bigger than France or Texas

00:01:51.780 --> 00:01:54.280
it's divided into 3 territories

00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:56.440
Indonesian Kalimantan in the south

00:01:56.580 --> 00:01:58.380
which means burning weather island

00:01:58.490 --> 00:02:01.340
and Brunei and Malaysian Borneo in the north

00:02:05.940 --> 00:02:06.810
From the air

00:02:06.900 --> 00:02:09.040
I catch a glimpse of what the palm oil industry

00:02:09.040 --> 00:02:11.040
has done to this country

00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:20.700
I land in Sarawak

00:02:21.160 --> 00:02:24.220
a Malaysian state that’s seen massive deforestation.

00:02:41.540 --> 00:02:43.800
A land where activists get killed

00:02:44.010 --> 00:02:45.660
or disappear

00:02:46.640 --> 00:02:48.830
During a period of over fifty years

00:02:48.900 --> 00:02:51.220
the Malaysian government has overseen the destruction

00:02:51.220 --> 00:02:55.080
of the vast majority of Borneo’s primary rainforest

00:02:55.650 --> 00:02:58.460
selling it for billions of dollars as timber

00:02:59.320 --> 00:03:01.960
Half of all the world’s annual supply of tropical timber

00:03:01.960 --> 00:03:03.960
comes from Borneo

00:03:05.400 --> 00:03:08.330
and it’s replaced with rubber plantations

00:03:08.780 --> 00:03:11.090
and oil palm trees

00:03:12.860 --> 00:03:14.480
the products of which are now used

00:03:14.480 --> 00:03:18.350
in around half of all packaged supermarket goods.

00:03:37.870 --> 00:03:40.560
Very few national parks have been created in Sarawak

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:42.320
to protect what is considered to be

00:03:42.380 --> 00:03:46.250
the oldest and most diverse ecosystems on Earth

00:03:54.550 --> 00:03:57.530
hosting one third of all plant species on the planet

00:03:57.530 --> 00:04:01.180
and an estimated 3 million insect species

00:04:03.420 --> 00:04:06.270
Less than 10% of Sarawak’s primary rainforest

00:04:06.270 --> 00:04:08.270
remains intact

00:04:11.710 --> 00:04:13.690
and where the land still looks green

00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:17.470
it's mostly secondary forest growing for more timber

00:04:27.770 --> 00:04:29.740
The forest grows back so densely

00:04:29.800 --> 00:04:31.890
that it’s no longer habitable for its biggest

00:04:31.890 --> 00:04:33.700
and most endangered species

00:04:33.700 --> 00:04:36.510
like the orangutan and sun bears

00:04:41.920 --> 00:04:44.380
The Penan were the only tribe of hunters-gatherers

00:04:44.380 --> 00:04:45.860
able to live as nomads

00:04:45.860 --> 00:04:48.370
in the primary rainforest of Borneo

00:04:49.660 --> 00:04:52.540
settling for a couple of months in temporary huts,

00:04:52.680 --> 00:04:54.530
collecting only what they needed

00:04:54.540 --> 00:04:56.770
before moving on to other hunting grounds

00:05:02.140 --> 00:05:04.380
A peaceful, good-natured people

00:05:04.510 --> 00:05:06.540
the 15,000 Penan who lived here

00:05:06.580 --> 00:05:07.620
didn’t stand a chance

00:05:07.620 --> 00:05:09.320
against the powerful logging companies

00:05:09.320 --> 00:05:12.040
supported by a mafia state government

00:05:13.470 --> 00:05:15.520
When they started blocking logging roads,

00:05:15.610 --> 00:05:18.330
many were arrested and even tortured.

00:05:19.000 --> 00:05:21.040
Most of them ended up in cities,

00:05:21.210 --> 00:05:23.540
forgetting their precious survival knowledge.

00:05:25.210 --> 00:05:27.000
Today, only a few families

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:29.540
have kept their traditional way of life.

00:05:29.860 --> 00:05:32.200
But as crucial space is missing,

00:05:32.700 --> 00:05:35.920
they are forced to live part-time in camps and villages.

00:05:42.330 --> 00:05:46.010
Bala is a semi-nomadic Penan tribesman.

00:05:46.630 --> 00:05:48.910
He’s recently had to take shelter in a village

00:05:48.910 --> 00:05:51.050
when his forest was cut down.

00:05:52.700 --> 00:05:55.360
He's using a bamboo segment as a container,

00:05:55.700 --> 00:05:58.220
to fill with the deadly Tajem,

00:05:58.710 --> 00:06:01.920
the poison applied to the tips of his hunting darts.

00:06:04.520 --> 00:06:06.210
The Tajem tree,

00:06:06.400 --> 00:06:08.430
or Antiaris Toxicaria,

00:06:08.550 --> 00:06:10.860
grows up to forty meters high.

00:06:12.790 --> 00:06:16.930
It’s widespread all-over south Asia and tropical Africa.

00:06:19.690 --> 00:06:21.780
In its raw form, the sap is deadly

00:06:21.830 --> 00:06:23.560
when it enters the bloodstream,

00:06:23.560 --> 00:06:26.060
causing lethal cardiac arrhythmia

00:06:26.680 --> 00:06:29.130
but, through a special refining process,

00:06:29.220 --> 00:06:31.960
the penan make it even more powerful.

00:06:34.550 --> 00:06:37.250
It is then fatal, in less than two minutes,

00:06:37.370 --> 00:06:39.850
depending on the size of the prey.

00:06:43.570 --> 00:06:45.450
It’s ideal for hunting

00:06:45.530 --> 00:06:48.250
as it doesn't contaminate the meat

00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:17.040
The Penan rely on foreign trade to procure iron

00:07:17.260 --> 00:07:21.210
in return for resin, animal skins and handicrafts.

00:07:41.380 --> 00:07:42.850
Bala carefully cleans

00:07:42.850 --> 00:07:46.030
the residual deadly sap from his machete

00:08:20.420 --> 00:08:22.350
Bala pours the harvested latex

00:08:22.410 --> 00:08:25.000
into a pot made of dried leaves.

00:08:28.990 --> 00:08:31.570
These pots are also made from green leaves

00:08:31.570 --> 00:08:33.940
for boiling water and cooking meat.

00:08:37.730 --> 00:08:38.850
He is careful to heat it

00:08:38.890 --> 00:08:40.900
at just the right distance from the flame

00:08:41.010 --> 00:08:42.900
to avoid boiling the sap.

00:08:45.170 --> 00:08:46.750
As night falls

00:08:46.900 --> 00:08:49.200
on the forest of Borneo

00:08:49.480 --> 00:08:53.070
many species risk leaving the safety of their burrows.

00:12:28.620 --> 00:12:31.120
To make the poison more powerful,

00:12:31.200 --> 00:12:33.580
the Penan follow a special recipe.

00:12:39.160 --> 00:12:42.700
They combine several plants in specific proportions.

00:12:52.160 --> 00:12:55.580
The darts are then coated in the poisonous mixture,

00:12:56.130 --> 00:12:58.330
dried by the fire,

00:12:59.130 --> 00:13:00.920
and are ready to use.

00:13:04.660 --> 00:13:07.380
Richard is a sedentary Penan.

00:13:07.710 --> 00:13:09.970
He was born ten years after his family

00:13:10.040 --> 00:13:12.590
was forced out of the jungle in the nineties.

00:13:13.690 --> 00:13:16.570
He works in a nearby national park.

00:13:16.950 --> 00:13:19.810
He's from one of the few villages that still has access

00:13:19.890 --> 00:13:21.860
to the untouched rainforest

00:13:21.980 --> 00:13:23.290
and reserve the right

00:13:23.340 --> 00:13:26.000
to practice traditional blowpipe hunting.

00:13:30.170 --> 00:13:32.060
He's joined by his cousin.

00:13:33.680 --> 00:13:36.010
They search for their favorite meat,

00:13:36.180 --> 00:13:37.840
the wild boar.

00:13:43.660 --> 00:13:45.420
It’s become harder to find

00:13:45.490 --> 00:13:46.780
due to human activities

00:13:46.780 --> 00:13:49.020
around the border of the park.

00:13:52.250 --> 00:13:53.530
Blowpipe hunting

00:13:53.560 --> 00:13:56.130
is a matter of stealth and patience.

00:13:59.330 --> 00:14:00.580
During the day

00:14:00.670 --> 00:14:02.820
every animal is on their guard.

00:14:31.300 --> 00:14:33.070
This squirrel got lucky.

00:14:51.260 --> 00:14:54.430
Richie has found some bamboo shoots.

00:14:58.410 --> 00:15:01.550
They will provide a vegetarian meal for tonight.

00:15:13.270 --> 00:15:14.730
Later, they’re boiled

00:15:14.730 --> 00:15:17.930
to clear the cyanide it naturally contains.

00:15:27.010 --> 00:15:29.460
The rivers running through the national parks

00:15:29.580 --> 00:15:32.260
are clear and free of pollution from logging,

00:15:32.380 --> 00:15:33.730
which erodes the ground

00:15:33.780 --> 00:15:35.730
and makes the water muddy.

00:15:36.060 --> 00:15:38.830
The Penan can still practice fishing here.

00:15:40.950 --> 00:15:43.420
They use homemade spear-guns.

00:16:37.870 --> 00:16:41.210
They also fish in swamps, using bread.

00:16:43.370 --> 00:16:46.540
It takes only three seconds to catch a fish.

00:17:00.830 --> 00:17:02.530
Now it’s my turn to try.

00:17:16.190 --> 00:17:19.280
A few Penan families still live as semi-nomads

00:17:19.280 --> 00:17:20.660
in the remote jungle.

00:17:21.600 --> 00:17:23.640
Langub, a sedentary Penan

00:17:23.680 --> 00:17:25.040
guides me to meet them.

00:17:32.650 --> 00:17:34.540
He needs the help of a Penan couple

00:17:34.540 --> 00:17:36.030
who live on the way.

00:18:20.590 --> 00:18:22.240
We arrive at a camp

00:18:24.480 --> 00:18:26.290
built with the help of some sedentary Penan

00:18:26.290 --> 00:18:27.960
to facilitate meetings

00:18:28.050 --> 00:18:29.620
between the nomads and their relatives

00:18:29.620 --> 00:18:31.010
who have left the forest.

00:18:34.860 --> 00:18:37.480
Saya lives here a couple of months a year.

00:18:38.560 --> 00:18:39.950
With his two sons,

00:18:40.020 --> 00:18:41.080
Tyon and Ajai,

00:18:41.930 --> 00:18:42.830
his wife

00:18:43.480 --> 00:18:44.960
and his two daughters.

00:18:45.840 --> 00:18:49.100
The Penan keep female names secret to outsiders,

00:18:49.390 --> 00:18:51.010
so, they’ll remain a mystery.

00:18:54.880 --> 00:18:55.760
For lunch,

00:18:56.000 --> 00:18:57.070
there’s rice,

00:18:57.180 --> 00:18:58.260
turtle

00:18:58.260 --> 00:19:01.370
and a mix of tapioca flour the Penan call Naon,

00:19:01.690 --> 00:19:03.200
which tastes like water.

00:19:14.460 --> 00:19:15.860
Their main source of food

00:19:15.950 --> 00:19:18.980
would traditionally come from the sago palm tree.

00:19:20.190 --> 00:19:23.580
Saya and his kids are taking me to harvest its trunk.

00:19:26.460 --> 00:19:29.440
The Penan take great care to not damage its roots,

00:19:29.700 --> 00:19:31.440
so the palm will continue to thrive.

00:19:34.670 --> 00:19:37.600
He first collects some moss called Jakah,

00:19:38.680 --> 00:19:40.700
an essential survival element

00:19:40.970 --> 00:19:42.700
used to light fires.

00:19:57.600 --> 00:20:00.290
The palm heart is typically made into a nutritious,

00:20:00.350 --> 00:20:02.290
non-perishable flour,

00:20:03.150 --> 00:20:04.930
but it's also eaten raw.

00:20:08.830 --> 00:20:10.670
At the junction of two paths,

00:20:10.840 --> 00:20:12.670
Saya shows me Oroo,

00:20:13.390 --> 00:20:14.750
the Penan’s way of communicating

00:20:14.780 --> 00:20:16.510
with other members of the tribe,

00:20:16.890 --> 00:20:18.670
using signs made of branches.

00:20:32.450 --> 00:20:35.340
The code always begins with the Penan motto,

00:20:35.400 --> 00:20:36.540
Jah Kenin

00:20:36.710 --> 00:20:38.540
“We have the same heart”

00:21:35.780 --> 00:21:38.350
In the few villages bordering reserved areas,

00:21:38.460 --> 00:21:40.030
the Penan have been offered jobs

00:21:40.030 --> 00:21:41.650
in the national parks

00:21:41.900 --> 00:21:43.180
and are allowed to hunt

00:21:43.180 --> 00:21:45.580
in untouched regions of rainforest.

00:22:04.350 --> 00:22:06.450
In tourist places like these,

00:22:06.510 --> 00:22:08.430
the government appears to have made an effort

00:22:08.480 --> 00:22:10.010
to build a brand-new hospital,

00:22:10.530 --> 00:22:12.210
but as I'll learn later,

00:22:12.340 --> 00:22:14.140
nothing has been arranged for the Penan

00:22:14.140 --> 00:22:15.600
who’ve been forced to settle down

00:22:15.600 --> 00:22:17.010
in remote villages.

00:22:19.960 --> 00:22:21.840
Like most tribes in Borneo,

00:22:21.930 --> 00:22:24.270
Penan families share living accommodation,

00:22:24.430 --> 00:22:26.040
called longhouses.

00:22:33.880 --> 00:22:36.130
Their woven craft is made of rattan,

00:22:36.380 --> 00:22:39.410
a vine that can reach a length of more than 200 meters

00:22:39.540 --> 00:22:42.210
and can be crushed to use as a natural toothbrush.

00:22:43.770 --> 00:22:46.260
It’s now being replaced by plastic.

00:22:47.630 --> 00:22:49.490
The Penan used to be animist,

00:22:49.600 --> 00:22:51.080
worshipping the forest spirit

00:22:51.220 --> 00:22:52.340
called Ballei

00:22:56.720 --> 00:22:58.560
Like most tribes in Borneo,

00:22:58.670 --> 00:23:00.560
they’ve been Christianized.

00:23:13.190 --> 00:23:15.450
Nyapun was one of the first Penan chiefs

00:23:15.450 --> 00:23:17.450
to be forced to settle down.

00:23:19.230 --> 00:23:21.580
He says he's probably 89 years old.

00:23:22.520 --> 00:23:24.600
The Penan used to live in the present.

00:23:25.180 --> 00:23:27.150
The canopy would hide the sky

00:23:27.200 --> 00:23:29.710
so they couldn’t count moon phases or years.

00:23:31.210 --> 00:23:32.780
He is gathering fern shoots

00:23:32.780 --> 00:23:34.780
and tapioca leaves for lunch.

00:23:46.070 --> 00:23:48.330
Like most tribesmen in rural Borneo,

00:23:48.600 --> 00:23:51.130
Nyapun never leaves his house without a weapon

00:23:51.180 --> 00:23:52.640
for self-defense.

00:23:55.710 --> 00:23:58.300
The spear attached to the end of his blowpipe

00:23:58.430 --> 00:24:00.400
is a reliable secondary weapon

00:24:01.770 --> 00:24:03.960
Tribal wars are still in living memory

00:24:04.250 --> 00:24:05.920
and encountering wild animals

00:24:05.980 --> 00:24:08.080
like giant pythons and sun bears

00:24:08.170 --> 00:24:09.630
could still be a deadly.

00:24:14.400 --> 00:24:15.720
Nyapun's second wife

00:24:15.800 --> 00:24:17.040
plays nose flute.

00:24:26.840 --> 00:24:28.120
They live in this compound

00:24:28.120 --> 00:24:30.180
with their children and grandchildren.

00:25:54.800 --> 00:25:57.590
Two villagers guide me to a remote Penan camp

00:25:57.620 --> 00:25:59.400
at the end of a logging road.

00:26:01.030 --> 00:26:03.070
These dangerous, abandoned roads

00:26:03.240 --> 00:26:05.800
are prone to landslides and sinkholes.

00:26:15.550 --> 00:26:17.620
A few pockets of primary forest

00:26:17.730 --> 00:26:19.620
were spared from the chainsaws.

00:26:38.680 --> 00:26:40.760
Penan are known to be very shy,

00:26:40.850 --> 00:26:42.840
which makes their adaptation to the outside

00:26:42.980 --> 00:26:44.250
very challenging.

00:26:51.410 --> 00:26:53.530
The villagers have a hornbill as a pet.

00:27:01.040 --> 00:27:03.540
Hornbills are the emblem of Sarawak.

00:27:06.040 --> 00:27:09.050
Many different species exist all over Asia.

00:27:09.520 --> 00:27:11.050
Like the Great Hornbill,

00:27:11.140 --> 00:27:13.050
here filmed in Cambodia.

00:27:43.200 --> 00:27:45.450
Older Penan folk suffer most

00:27:45.570 --> 00:27:47.650
from their lost nomadic lifestyle,

00:27:51.880 --> 00:27:54.730
and struggle to adapt to life in the villages.

00:27:57.430 --> 00:27:59.550
Many suffer from depression

00:27:59.790 --> 00:28:02.350
or have lost their minds.

00:28:21.960 --> 00:28:23.770
I'm back with Bala.

00:28:24.800 --> 00:28:27.710
He shows me how to make Penan barkcloth.

00:28:30.100 --> 00:28:31.960
He cuts a Beripun tree

00:28:45.220 --> 00:28:46.350
and beats it

00:28:46.470 --> 00:28:48.580
to remove the bark from the trunk.

00:29:02.030 --> 00:29:05.460
The Penan use over 1,500 names for plants.

00:29:06.080 --> 00:29:08.660
Most of them are used in their ethnobotany.

00:29:25.930 --> 00:29:27.380
He softens the bark

00:29:27.490 --> 00:29:28.880
by beating it.

00:29:39.520 --> 00:29:42.120
These were the traditional loin-cloths

00:29:42.600 --> 00:29:44.280
until cotton replaced them.

00:29:51.720 --> 00:29:55.370
This plant is used by the Penan to treat snake bites.

00:29:55.670 --> 00:29:58.100
It's one of the most useful medicinal plants

00:29:58.130 --> 00:29:59.480
in the jungle of Borneo,

00:29:59.600 --> 00:30:01.840
which is loaded with deadly snakes.

00:30:44.000 --> 00:30:45.080
This same plant

00:30:45.140 --> 00:30:47.560
saved the life of Swiss ethnologist and activist

00:30:47.600 --> 00:30:49.010
Bruno Manser.

00:30:49.170 --> 00:30:51.570
He spent six years living with the Penan

00:30:51.570 --> 00:30:52.790
in the eighties,

00:30:52.830 --> 00:30:55.220
unifying and rallying them against the loggers

00:30:55.300 --> 00:30:57.590
by helping to block logging roads.

00:30:57.780 --> 00:31:00.550
The Malaysian government put a price on his head.

00:31:01.150 --> 00:31:04.020
He disappeared in the jungle of Sarawak in 2000,

00:31:04.190 --> 00:31:06.210
following his return from Europe.

00:31:06.500 --> 00:31:09.030
He was declared dead in 2005

00:31:09.220 --> 00:31:11.300
and remains as a hero for the Penan.

00:31:16.460 --> 00:31:18.040
Though I hope I won't need it,

00:31:18.430 --> 00:31:21.230
I decide to keep some for the rest of my trip.

00:31:28.980 --> 00:31:31.800
Another very powerful medicinal plant in Borneo

00:31:31.880 --> 00:31:33.400
is Kratom.

00:31:34.400 --> 00:31:37.830
It's known to treat opiate and alcohol addiction.

00:31:38.580 --> 00:31:40.690
It's also used against fever

00:31:40.960 --> 00:31:43.370
and to relieve stress and anxiety.

00:31:51.690 --> 00:31:54.030
I now travel to the south of Sarawak,

00:31:54.190 --> 00:31:55.720
near the Indonesian border,

00:31:55.770 --> 00:31:57.080
to meet the Iban,

00:31:57.320 --> 00:31:59.600
who are known as the head hunters.

00:32:07.040 --> 00:32:08.980
Their name is also the Lakya

00:32:09.050 --> 00:32:10.980
which means “the strong men”.

00:32:13.600 --> 00:32:16.210
They used to practice head hunting on other tribes,

00:32:16.310 --> 00:32:17.730
including the Penan,

00:32:19.450 --> 00:32:21.940
and some families still keep skulls

00:32:21.970 --> 00:32:24.070
as trophies in their longhouses.

00:32:33.800 --> 00:32:36.550
The Iban don't use blowpipes for hunting,

00:32:36.710 --> 00:32:38.690
opting for regular guns.

00:32:41.310 --> 00:32:44.810
Even though head hunting officially ceased a century ago,

00:32:44.990 --> 00:32:48.120
the Iban are still remembered as merciless warriors,

00:32:50.690 --> 00:32:52.790
and guides still advise against entering

00:32:52.840 --> 00:32:55.410
Iban longhouses without invitation.

00:33:14.850 --> 00:33:16.930
They are famous for their tattoos.

00:33:36.400 --> 00:33:38.880
Tattoos represent an important life event

00:33:38.990 --> 00:33:40.510
like becoming a father.

00:33:42.660 --> 00:33:44.410
For the first head taken,

00:33:44.460 --> 00:33:46.410
a tattoo was made on the hand

00:33:46.800 --> 00:33:48.520
the Entegulun

00:33:55.130 --> 00:33:57.610
The Iban now mix their traditional tattoos

00:33:57.610 --> 00:33:59.610
with Western styles

00:34:00.480 --> 00:34:03.480
and foreign travelers come to get Iban tattoos.

00:34:09.060 --> 00:34:10.480
At Saya's camp,

00:34:10.920 --> 00:34:12.720
the family is preparing for a trip

00:34:12.720 --> 00:34:13.860
through the jungle.

00:34:19.060 --> 00:34:21.850
Saya's daughter is petting the family's monkey.

00:34:23.900 --> 00:34:25.720
The Penan use them as guards,

00:34:25.800 --> 00:34:28.080
giving a signal when danger is near.

00:34:52.830 --> 00:34:54.990
The family prepares to build a camp

00:34:55.330 --> 00:34:57.640
en route to their next living place.

00:35:13.340 --> 00:35:15.090
They choose an uphill spot

00:35:15.170 --> 00:35:18.110
away from mosquitoes and falling trees.

00:35:31.830 --> 00:35:34.230
Under such changeable weather conditions,

00:35:34.330 --> 00:35:36.960
they must be able to build a rainproof shelter

00:35:37.120 --> 00:35:38.810
in under an hour.

00:35:53.990 --> 00:35:56.020
As part of Penan fashion,

00:35:56.100 --> 00:35:57.750
they sometimes like to dye their hands

00:35:57.800 --> 00:35:59.240
with red leaves.

00:37:48.150 --> 00:37:50.260
The family occasionally leave the forest

00:37:50.350 --> 00:37:52.260
to trade meat in the villages,

00:37:52.870 --> 00:37:55.140
but they're often isolated for months

00:37:55.280 --> 00:37:58.420
and must light their fires without using a lighter.

00:37:59.650 --> 00:38:00.790
With his knife,

00:38:00.880 --> 00:38:03.960
Saya strikes a firestone that he found in the river.

00:38:06.740 --> 00:38:08.480
Sparks fall on the moss,

00:38:08.560 --> 00:38:10.120
igniting it.

00:38:20.790 --> 00:38:22.800
More moss and a bit of cloth

00:38:22.960 --> 00:38:24.800
help the flames grow.

00:38:41.210 --> 00:38:42.900
To illuminate the hut at night,

00:38:42.980 --> 00:38:44.900
the Penan use a plant candle,

00:38:44.930 --> 00:38:46.900
made from the pod of a vine.

00:38:47.270 --> 00:38:50.160
It provides light for up to an hour.

00:38:53.710 --> 00:38:56.500
It rains almost every day in the jungle.

00:38:57.540 --> 00:39:00.310
Punyau, a readily-combustible wood,

00:39:00.450 --> 00:39:03.200
is cut and briefly dried above the fire.

00:39:07.760 --> 00:39:10.290
Tyon is making Penan forks,

00:39:10.510 --> 00:39:12.190
or Atip,

00:39:12.660 --> 00:39:15.300
some versatile and effective cutlery,

00:39:15.600 --> 00:39:17.810
which are easy and fast to craft.

00:39:43.200 --> 00:39:44.600
I leave the nomads

00:39:44.650 --> 00:39:46.160
to meet a displaced community

00:39:46.230 --> 00:39:47.910
at the center of Sarawak.

00:39:48.720 --> 00:39:50.440
There is no public transport,

00:39:50.550 --> 00:39:52.440
so, I decide to hitchhike.

00:40:17.410 --> 00:40:20.400
The village is hours away from any other camp

00:40:21.090 --> 00:40:24.400
and I’m travelling next to 1000 liters of diesel.

00:40:54.660 --> 00:40:56.590
I come with a present,

00:40:57.040 --> 00:40:59.430
the book written by Bruno Manser.

00:41:11.280 --> 00:41:13.070
They can't read it,

00:41:13.580 --> 00:41:15.160
but it contains many drawings

00:41:15.220 --> 00:41:17.830
of what are now rare animals and plants.

00:42:16.470 --> 00:42:19.130
Some villagers are preparing their blowpipes

00:42:19.200 --> 00:42:21.130
for hunting the next day.

00:42:34.830 --> 00:42:37.810
Penan blowpipes are crafted from ironwood,

00:42:37.920 --> 00:42:40.040
the hardest wood in the jungle.

00:42:40.520 --> 00:42:43.010
Their making is a long process,

00:42:43.260 --> 00:42:45.730
drilling by hand on a platform.

00:42:46.370 --> 00:42:47.960
The use of wooden blowpipes

00:42:48.020 --> 00:42:50.510
only appeared in the middle of the 20th century

00:42:50.820 --> 00:42:52.960
when the Penan acquired iron drills.

00:42:53.370 --> 00:42:55.830
They were previously made of bamboo.

00:42:56.390 --> 00:42:57.840
Once shaped as a tube

00:42:57.900 --> 00:42:59.990
and sanded down with a special leaf,

00:43:00.180 --> 00:43:03.050
the blowpipe is precise up to forty meters.

00:43:11.370 --> 00:43:12.790
Tribesmen in Borneo

00:43:12.860 --> 00:43:15.110
have developed a very distinctive style

00:43:16.210 --> 00:43:17.680
combining modern outfits

00:43:17.790 --> 00:43:20.480
with traditional survival gear and jewelry

00:43:22.150 --> 00:43:24.740
High socks help protect against leeches.

00:43:28.500 --> 00:43:30.370
They wear rattan bracelets

00:43:30.410 --> 00:43:32.370
and wild boar tusks.

00:43:36.720 --> 00:43:38.710
Apart from an unused room

00:43:38.800 --> 00:43:40.340
presented as a school

00:43:40.430 --> 00:43:43.350
the government has done nothing for these families,

00:43:43.540 --> 00:43:45.140
in the hopes that they will eventually

00:43:45.140 --> 00:43:46.900
migrate to the cities.

00:43:47.710 --> 00:43:49.850
The head of the camp wants to talk to me

00:43:49.910 --> 00:43:51.850
about the situation here.

00:44:32.210 --> 00:44:33.560
Sarawak is home

00:44:33.630 --> 00:44:35.370
to what was once thought to be the largest

00:44:35.390 --> 00:44:36.560
cave on earth,

00:44:37.750 --> 00:44:41.230
until one bigger was recently discovered in Vietnam.

00:44:59.430 --> 00:45:01.280
Three and a half million bats

00:45:01.360 --> 00:45:03.280
make their home inside.

00:45:05.950 --> 00:45:07.600
Every evening,

00:45:07.700 --> 00:45:10.920
they leave the cave in search of fruit and insects.

00:45:17.190 --> 00:45:18.530
They form swarms

00:45:18.660 --> 00:45:20.980
to confuse predators like eagles.

00:46:18.320 --> 00:46:20.170
After nightfall in the camp,

00:46:20.870 --> 00:46:22.240
the families gather

00:46:22.460 --> 00:46:24.740
to watch the only Kung Fu movie they have.

00:46:39.550 --> 00:46:40.980
This giant cicada

00:46:41.120 --> 00:46:43.410
is one of a thousand different cicada species

00:46:43.440 --> 00:46:44.410
in Borneo

00:46:57.220 --> 00:46:58.350
In the morning,

00:46:58.510 --> 00:47:00.810
the village kids get ready for hunting.

00:47:14.130 --> 00:47:15.630
Lia, Asik,

00:47:15.760 --> 00:47:18.350
and their little brothers Yung and Jokim

00:47:18.660 --> 00:47:20.400
find a thorny palm

00:47:21.710 --> 00:47:24.610
whose soft heart is used for the darts’ fletching.

00:47:26.870 --> 00:47:28.720
Without this crucial element,

00:47:28.820 --> 00:47:30.900
their entire survival in the jungle

00:47:31.000 --> 00:47:32.710
would be jeopardized.

00:47:34.910 --> 00:47:36.970
The plug is carved to the right width

00:47:37.110 --> 00:47:38.640
using a wooden guide.

00:48:07.000 --> 00:48:08.550
The season for wild fruit

00:48:08.660 --> 00:48:10.450
lasts only three months a year,

00:48:11.070 --> 00:48:13.810
and boars have become rare in this secondary forest.

00:48:14.930 --> 00:48:16.890
As a result, the Penan are forced

00:48:16.930 --> 00:48:18.890
to rely on supplementary foods,

00:48:19.100 --> 00:48:21.220
like birds and pygmy squirrels.

00:48:45.230 --> 00:48:48.710
Using the poison, small prey dies instantly.

00:49:02.690 --> 00:49:04.170
Yung has found a plant

00:49:04.340 --> 00:49:06.410
used by kids as hair gel.

00:49:10.390 --> 00:49:13.610
Stingless bees come to drink on our skin.

00:49:30.010 --> 00:49:32.910
Giant wild bees also make swarms

00:49:32.980 --> 00:49:35.140
on the remaining tall trees.

00:50:09.210 --> 00:50:12.130
The Penan use a distinctive tongue clicking

00:50:12.510 --> 00:50:14.810
to express surprise and approval

00:50:46.290 --> 00:50:47.700
It’s time for me

00:50:47.840 --> 00:50:49.700
to leave the Penan.

00:50:50.750 --> 00:50:52.560
A people for whom kindness,

00:50:52.600 --> 00:50:54.230
generosity and courage

00:50:54.260 --> 00:50:55.780
is a way of life.

00:50:57.330 --> 00:50:58.520
Their ingenuity,

00:50:58.590 --> 00:51:00.650
and the elegant solutions they employ

00:51:00.650 --> 00:51:02.810
to thrive in some of the most challenging

00:51:02.930 --> 00:51:04.710
and complex environments

00:51:04.750 --> 00:51:06.400
must not be lost.

00:51:09.200 --> 00:51:10.440
All of humanity

00:51:10.470 --> 00:51:12.690
could benefit from Penan knowledge of plants

00:51:12.820 --> 00:51:14.870
and their understanding of nature.

00:51:18.820 --> 00:51:19.830
One day

00:51:19.990 --> 00:51:21.570
the tables will turn,

00:51:21.750 --> 00:51:23.320
and the wealth of every country

00:51:23.420 --> 00:51:24.350
will be defined

00:51:24.470 --> 00:51:26.080
by the nature they hold

00:51:27.650 --> 00:51:29.110
when that day comes,

00:51:29.310 --> 00:51:31.760
the last Penan families living in the wild

00:51:32.080 --> 00:51:33.990
may have left the rainforest

00:51:35.010 --> 00:51:35.970
along

00:51:36.390 --> 00:51:38.050
with all their secrets.	

